Wrapping a DOM element in SNGL is what maps the names. The standard library
calls the event `@scrollEnd`; the DOM calls it `scrollend`; the override that
wraps the element maps one to the other. The element's wildcard event stays
lowercase, because that is the set of DOM event types -- accepting the SNGL
spelling there would emit a listener for an event nothing fires.

`@longPress` has no DOM event of its own. `contextmenu` is what a sustained
touch press fires, and stands in until html synthesizes the gesture.

deny out/none/html/index.html `addEventListener\("scrollEnd"` -- the DOM event type is scrollend, so a listener under the SNGL spelling never fires
deny out/none/html/index.html `addEventListener\("longPress"` -- no DOM event is named longPress; contextmenu is what the gesture registers as

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

component main node {
    var n = 0
    scroll(@scrollEnd { n = n + 1 }) {
        text(value=string(n))
    }
    button(text="hit", @longPress { n = 0 }, @click { n = n + 1 })
}

window {
    main
}
